<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khudabad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">18th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khudabad is a ruined town near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalhora-dynasty">Kalhora dynasty</a> in the early 18th century. It served as the Kalhora capital in the middle of the century, with a large congregational mosque whose remains still stand. The capital later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a>, founded in 1768, and Khudabad declined.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khudabad.jpg" alt="Khudabad" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shakeel Ahmed · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gate_of_Jamia_Mosque_(Khudabad).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Khudabad lies in Dadu District of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, south of the district capital Dadu, at an altitude of 31 metres. The region had become mainly Muslim over the centuries after the conquest of Sindh by Muhammad bin Qasim, through the work of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> saints whose shrines dot the landscape. The Jamia Masjid, also called the Badshahi Masjid, was built during the rule of Yar Muhammad Kalhoro and served as a school and for military training as well as a mosque; Yar Muhammad's tomb also stands at Khudabad.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Yar Muhammad Kalhoro died around 1718 or 1719 and was buried at Khudabad. His successor Noor Mohammad Kalhoro, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> from 1719 to 1755, chose it as his capital and developed it quickly, and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> of the Amil and Bhaiband castes came there for work and trade. Noor Mohammad was buried in a tomb he had built about a kilometre from the town, where mourners left sticks as a mark of respect, a custom Hindus also kept in ceremonies for their children.</p><p>After his death the claim of Ghulam Shah Kalhoro was challenged by his brothers Muradyab Khan and Attar Khan, and Attar Khan, who had obtained a grant of authority from Ahmed Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani</a>, briefly took the seat but could not keep order. The Khosa tribe then raided the area and burned Khudabad, by one account by tying lit fire crackers to the feet of birds and releasing them over the city. In 1759 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baloch">Baloch</a> chiefs restored Ghulam Shah, who settled Khudabad again on new land by the new course of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>.</p><p>In 1783 the Talpur leader Fateh Ali Khan defeated the Kalhora ruler Abdul Nabi and took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> flooded Khudabad, the court moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a>, the old Nayrun Kot, much of the population followed, and the town's decline began. People are said to find old coins among the ruins: around 1908 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charcoal">charcoal</a> seller found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins worth about thirteen thousand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a> there and was charged under the Indian Treasure-Trove Act.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Khudabad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Kalhoras built canals and founded towns, including <b>Khudabad</b> and Hyderabad.</blockquote><a href="/state/kalhora-dynasty">Kalhora dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
